E["npc:chronormu"] = {t="npc",n="Chronormu",z="westernplaguelands",s="Chronormu, who goes by the mortal name Chromie, is a bronze dragon disguised as an energetic female gnome. Tasked with safeguarding the flow of time on Azeroth, she often works directly with mortals rather than observing from afar. In the Western Plaguelands, she quietly intervenes in the tragic aftermath of the Third War to right disrupted temporal threads.",h="Chronormu, who goes by the mortal name Chromie, is a bronze dragon disguised as an energetic female gnome.",sec={{t="A Dragon in Small Guise",b="Though she appears to mortals as an eccentric and cheerful gnome named Chromie, Chronormu is a member of the bronze dragonflight serving under the Timeless One, Nozdormu. Bronze dragons are charged by the titans with guarding history and correcting disruptions in the timeways. When deciding on her mortal visage, Chronormu sought counsel from dragons across the flights, including Kalecgos of the blue flight and Onyxia of the black. Ultimately rejecting notions of power or dominion, she chose the form of a female gnome so she could walk beside mortals as an approachable, optimistic peer.",sp=0},{t="Watcher of the Plaguelands",b="Chronormu has taken up residence within the ruined ruins of Lordaeron, particularly watching the devastated Western Plaguelands. The catastrophic downfall of the kingdom and the spread of the Scourge tore deeply into the region's history, leaving temporal anomalies and trapped remnants. Working discreetly from places like the ruins of Andorhal, she enlists mortal adventurers to mend tangled timelines and investigate disturbances without revealing the full terrifying majesty of the dragonflight unless necessary.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Is Chromie really a gnome?",a="No. Chromie is merely the mortal visage of Chronormu, a bronze dragon. Bronze dragons adopt mortal guises to walk among the younger races without drawing undue terror or suspicion.",al={"is chromie a gnome","chromie true form","who is chromie"}},{q="Why does Chronormu have a male dragon name?",a="Among bronze dragons, names ending in '-ormu' are traditionally given to males, while female bronze dragons typically have names ending in '-ormi'. Chronormu was born male, but during her Visage Day ceremony she chose to define herself as a female gnome, explaining simply that the form suited her.",al={"chronormu name gender","why is chromie called chronormu","chronormu gender"}},{q="What is Chromie doing in the Western Plaguelands?",a="The devastation caused by the Scourge in Lordaeron caused severe disruptions to history and the timeways. Chromie is stationed in the ruins to investigate temporal anomalies, undo historical disturbances, and enlist trustworthy adventurers to repair damaged threads of time.",al={"what is chromie doing here","why is chromie in andorhal","chromie western plaguelands"}},{q="Who are the bronze dragonflight?",a="Led by the Dragon Aspect Nozdormu, the bronze dragonflight was blessed by the titans with the power to travel through and protect the pathways of time. They ensure that history unfolds as intended and intervene when time is altered or corrupted.",al={"bronze dragons","what is bronze flight","nozdormu flight"}}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Chronormu deliberately chose a gnome visage because she admired their buoyant optimism, inventiveness, and unassuming nature when walking among larger races.",["class:Mage"]="Mages studying the arcane discipline of chronomancy will find Chronormu's casual manipulation of time and localized temporal pockets both fascinating and staggering in scale."},kw={"andorhal","bronze dragon","chromie","chronormu","nozdormu","timewalker","visage"},rel={"zone:westernplaguelands","npc:kalecgos","npc:onyxia","topic:gnome","topic:black-dragonflight"},src={"Chronormu"}}
E["npc:darkmaster-gandling"] = {t="npc",n="Darkmaster Gandling",z="westernplaguelands",s="Darkmaster Gandling is the mortal headmaster of Scholomance, overseeing the instruction and training of necromancers for the Cult of the Damned. Operating from the depths of the ruined Keep of Caer Darrow, he serves the Scourge by orchestrating gruesome magical experiments upon the living and dead alike.",h="Darkmaster Gandling is the mortal headmaster of Scholomance, overseeing the instruction and training of...",sec={{t="The Headmaster's Domain",b="Within the dark halls beneath Caer Darrow, Darkmaster Gandling governs Scholomance, the premier academy for the Scourge's aspiring necromancers. A living human serving the Cult of the Damned, Gandling worked alongside the lich Ras Frostwhisper to orchestrate atrocities against countless innocent captives taken from the surrounding lands. Students who enter his classrooms are instructed in plague brewing, reanimation, and flesh sculpting. Appointed headmaster by Kel'Thuzad himself, Gandling answers directly to the Cult's founder.",sp=0},{t="Master of the Reliquary",b="Residing deep within the Headmaster's Study, Gandling commands the dark relics and spirits trapped inside the ancient school. He wields devastating arcane and shadow magic in conjunction with forbidden necromantic arts, cursing intruders and hurling them through shadow portals into the sealed chambers surrounding his study, where they must fight alone against the risen dead while Gandling shields himself from harm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Darkmaster Gandling?",a="Darkmaster Gandling is a human necromancer belonging to the Cult of the Damned. He acts as the headmaster of Scholomance, running the dark school and training new necromancers to serve the Lich King.",al={"who is gandling","darkmaster gandling lore","scholomance boss"}},{q="Is Gandling an undead or a living human?",a="Despite his mastery of death magic and allegiance to the Scourge, Gandling remains a living human mortal. He leads mortal acolytes and cultists within Scholomance, serving the Scourge willingly.",al={"is gandling undead","is gandling alive","gandling race"}},{q="What is his connection to Ras Frostwhisper?",a="Ras Frostwhisper was Gandling's teacher. Together, the human headmaster and the powerful lich established Scholomance as the center of dark instruction and supervised the horrors committed against innocent souls such as the Sarkhoffs within Caer Darrow.",al={"frostwhisper gandling","ras frostwhisper teacher"}},{q="Where is Darkmaster Gandling found?",a="Gandling resides in the Headmaster's Study at the very bottom of Scholomance, accessible through the catacombs beneath Caer Darrow in Darrowmere Lake.",al={"gandling location","where is gandling","how to find gandling"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Gandling represents the ultimate corruption of Dalaran's scholarly traditions, twisting academic rigor into a horrifying factory of undeath.",["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, Gandling is a hated overseer of Scourge indoctrination who binds free souls into eternal enslavement beneath Caer Darrow."},kw={"caer darrow","cult of the damned","darkmaster gandling","gandling","headmaster","necromancer","scholomance"},rel={"subzone:scholomance","zone:westernplaguelands","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:scourge","subzone:andorhal"},src={"Darkmaster Gandling"}}
E["npc:eva-sarkhoff"] = {t="npc",n="Eva Sarkhoff",z="westernplaguelands",s="Eva Sarkhoff is the spectral remnant of the former head maid of House Barov, lingering alongside her husband Lucien just outside the horrors of Scholomance. Tortured and slain by the Butcher during the fall of Caer Darrow, her spirit seeks brave mortals to avenge their deaths and destroy their cursed remains.",h="Eva Sarkhoff is the spectral remnant of the former head maid of House Barov, lingering alongside her husband...",sec={{t="The Fall of House Barov",b="Eva served the wealthy Barov family for decades as their head maid, caring for the sprawling keep of Caer Darrow alongside her husband Lucien, the family butler. As the Barovs grew paranoid about their aging mortality and vast wealth, they struck a catastrophic pact with Kel'Thuzad. The manor was gradually handed over to the fledgling Cult of the Damned, warping the grand residence into Scholomance, a secretive academy of necromancy. Eva and Lucien hid in the keep's corridors for six months, scavenging food while watching cultists and monstrosities steadily take over their home.",sp=0},{t="The Butcher's Scalpel",b="Inevitably discovered by the Scourge, Eva and Lucien were delivered into the custody of Doctor Theolen Krastinov, known as the Butcher. Krastinov subjected the pair to weeks of agonized experimentation, using dark magic to prolong their lives well past the threshold of mortal endurance. Even after death, their corpses were fed to ghouls while their restless spirits were chained to Caer Darrow, where Eva now begs passing adventurers to slay Krastinov and incinerate their remains.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who was Eva Sarkhoff before she died?",a="Eva Sarkhoff was the head maid for the noble Barov family, residing and working in the keep of Caer Darrow for decades alongside her husband, Lucien, who served as butler.",al={"eva sarkhoff backstory","who is eva sarkhoff","eva lore"}},{q="How did Eva die?",a="After hiding for months from the Cult of the Damned, Eva and Lucien were discovered and turned over to Doctor Theolen Krastinov. Krastinov relentlessly tortured them for weeks, keeping them alive with dark magic until they finally died and had their corpses fed to ghouls.",al={"how did eva sarkhoff die","eva sarkhoff death","who killed eva"}},{q="Why is Eva's ghost still in Caer Darrow?",a="Eva's torment was so horrific and unnatural that her spirit remains tied to the mortal plane. She cannot pass into eternity until Doctor Krastinov is slain and her physical remains inside Scholomance are burned.",al={"why is eva a ghost","eva spirit","why is eva lingering"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="Witnessing the lingering torment of Eva and Lucien is a stark reminder of the Scourge's cruelty, demanding that holy practitioners grant peace to their restless souls and cleanse the foul magic binding them.",["race:Human"]="Eva and her husband represent the ordinary servants of Lordaeron who were betrayed by their own corrupt aristocracy when House Barov sold Caer Darrow to Kel'Thuzad."},kw={"barov","caer darrow","cult of the damned","eva sarkhoff","krastinov","lucien sarkhoff","scholomance","the butcher"},rel={"subzone:caer-darrow","subzone:scholomance","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:scourge","zone:westernplaguelands","quest:1066"},src={"Eva Sarkhoff"}}
E["npc:koltira-deathweaver"] = {t="npc",n="Koltira Deathweaver",z="westernplaguelands",s="Koltira Deathweaver was a valiant high elf defender of Quel'Thalas who fell defending his homeland against the Scourge during the Third War. Raised into undeath by the Lich King's servants, he was transformed into an undead death knight bound in iron servitude to the Scourge.",h="Koltira Deathweaver was a valiant high elf defender of Quel'Thalas who fell defending his homeland against...",sec={{t="Fall of Quel'Thalas",b="In life, Koltira stood among the proud defenders of the high elven kingdom of Quel'Thalas. When Prince Arthas Menethil marched the Scourge north to reach the Sunwell during the Third War, Koltira and his kin fought desperately against overwhelming waves of undead monstrosities. Defending his homeland alongside his brother Faltora, Koltira faced the Scourge death knight Thassarian in combat. Koltira was ultimately defeated and slain when the elfgates fell and the Scourge overran the realm.",sp=0},{t="A Bound Death Knight",b="Following his death, Koltira was raised as an undead death knight under the absolute command of the Lich King. Stripped of his living allegiance, he became bound to the will of the Frozen Throne. Strangely, a bond formed between Koltira and Thassarian—the man who had struck him down—with the two regarding each other as brothers in undeath while serving within the dark ranks of the Scourge.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Koltira Deathweaver?",a="Koltira is a high elven defender who was killed during the Scourge invasion of Quel'Thalas in the Third War and subsequently raised as a death knight under the control of the Lich King.",al={"koltira lore","who is koltira","koltira deathweaver backstory"}},{q="How did Koltira die?",a="He was killed in single combat by the Scourge death knight Thassarian during the assault on Quel'Thalas after the kingdom's enchanted elfgates were breached.",al={"how did koltira die","koltira death","who killed koltira"}},{q="What is Koltira's connection to Thassarian?",a="Though Thassarian took Koltira's life in Silvermoon, Koltira felt internal conflict within the human death knight. Once Koltira was reanimated by the Scourge, the two warriors formed an unusual brotherly bond while serving the Lich King.",al={"thassarian and koltira","koltira thassarian relationship","why are they friends"}},{q="Which faction does Koltira serve right now?",a="At this time, Koltira remains under the dominion of the Lich King, serving as an undead death knight bound to the Scourge.",al={"koltira faction","is koltira scourge","is koltira horde"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Forsaken who remember the fall of Quel'Thalas know all too well the horror of being murdered and bound into servitude by the Scourge.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance veterans of the Third War remember the tragic collapse of Quel'Thalas and the terrible cost exacted by Prince Arthas."},kw={"death knight","deathweaver","high elf","koltira","quel'thalas","scourge","silvermoon","thassarian"},rel={"npc:thassarian","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","topic:third-war","topic:arthas-menethil","subzone:andorhal","zone:westernplaguelands"},src={"Koltira Deathweaver"}}
E["npc:thassarian"] = {t="npc",n="Thassarian",z="westernplaguelands",s="Thassarian was once an earnest footman of Lordaeron who followed Prince Arthas into Northrend during the Third War. Slain in the frozen wastes, he was raised as a death knight bound to the will of the Scourge.",h="Thassarian was once an earnest footman of Lordaeron who followed Prince Arthas into Northrend during the...",sec={{t="A Soldier of Lordaeron",b="Before the fall of the northern kingdoms, Thassarian was a dutiful farm boy who enlisted in Lordaeron's army to follow the example of his late father. Taught that military obedience was paramount, he obeyed orders without hesitation. That loyalty led him directly into horror: he accompanied Prince Arthas through the Culling of Stratholme and sailed north with the 1st Legion to hunt the dreadlord Mal'Ganis in the frozen wastes.",sp=0},{t="Servant of the Scourge",b="While searching for the vanished prince in Northrend, Thassarian stumbled into the cavern where Frostmourne had rested. There he was struck down by the fallen Captain Falric. Raised as a death knight under Arthas's dominion, Thassarian was forced to return to his homeland and murder his own mother, Vivian, to cement his subservience. Bound to the Lich King, he marches among the Scourge's dread commanders in the ruin of the north.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Thassarian?",a="Thassarian is a former human soldier from Lordaeron who marched with Prince Arthas to Northrend during the Third War. He was killed there and raised into undeath as a lieutenant and death knight of the Scourge.",al={"thassarian lore","who is thassarian","thassarian backstory"}},{q="How did Thassarian become a death knight?",a="While searching for Prince Arthas in Northrend, Thassarian entered Frostmourne Cavern and was killed by Captain Falric, who had already fallen to undeath. He was subsequently resurrected by Arthas as an undead death knight.",al={"thassarian death","how did thassarian die","who turned thassarian"}},{q="What terrible deed did the Scourge force him to commit?",a="Upon returning to Lordaeron with the Scourge, Arthas forced Thassarian to execute his own mother, Vivian, to extinguish his remnants of humanity and test his absolute obedience to the Lich King.",al={"thassarian mother","did thassarian kill his mom","thassarian vivian"}},{q="What is his connection to Koltira Deathweaver?",a="During the Scourge invasion of Quel'Thalas, Thassarian fought and killed the high elven defender Koltira. When Koltira was subsequently raised as a death knight, the two forged an enduring bond of brotherhood in undeath.",al={"thassarian and koltira","koltira thassarian friendship"}}},ang={["race:Human"]="Thassarian's fate represents the tragedy of Lordaeron's footmen, who marched north out of devotion to their prince only to be butchered and turned into monsters against their own families.",["class:Warrior"]="Trained by his father to believe that an obedient soldier wins wars, Thassarian followed martial discipline all the way into the freezing cavern that doomed his soul."},kw={"arthas","death knight","falric","koltira","lordaeron","scourge","stratholme","thassarian"},rel={"topic:arthas-menethil","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","subzone:stratholme","npc:koltira-deathweaver","topic:lordaeron","topic:third-war","zone:westernplaguelands"},src={"Thassarian"}}
E["subzone:andorhal"] = {t="subzone",n="Andorhal",z="westernplaguelands",s="Once the thriving agricultural heart of northern Lordaeron, Andorhal became ground zero for the Kingdom's destruction during the Third War. It was here that Kel'Thuzad tainted the grain with the Plague of Undeath, unleashing horror across the region. Today, the ruined town is held firmly in the iron grip of the Scourge.",h="Once the thriving agricultural heart of northern Lordaeron, Andorhal became ground zero for the Kingdom's...",sec={{t="Ground Zero of the Plague",b="Before the Third War, Andorhal was the prosperous granary of the Eastweald, gathering grain from across Lordaeron and distributing it eastward to Hearthglen and Stratholme. Because of this vast distribution network, Kel'Thuzad and the Cult of the Damned chose Andorhal as the staging ground for their horrific plot. By tainting the grain crates with the Plague of Undeath, the cult turned the breadbasket of the kingdom into an engine of necrosis, infecting countless citizens before the Crown even understood the threat.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of the Silver Hand",b="Prince Arthas Menethil and Jaina Proudmoore initially confronted and slew Kel'Thuzad here, but the necromancer's death was not the end. When Arthas returned from Northrend as a champion of the Scourge, he marched back to Andorhal to reclaim Kel'Thuzad's remains. Uther the Lightbringer and the remnants of the Silver Hand made a desperate last stand in defense of King Terenas's ashes. The paladins were overwhelmed, Uther was slain by his former pupil, and the grand settlement was reduced to a blight-choked Scourge stronghold.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is Andorhal so important to the plague's history?",a="Andorhal was the central agricultural distribution hub for northern Lordaeron. Kel'Thuzad exploited this supply chain, infesting the grain silos with the Plague of Undeath and shipping the tainted harvest along the King's Road directly to Stratholme and neighboring towns.",al={"how did plague start here","andorhal grain plague","why andorhal mattered"}},{q="Who controls Andorhal right now?",a="The undead Scourge firmly controls the ruins of Andorhal. Skeletal sentries, necromancers, and ghouls stalk the scorched streets, guarding the epicenter of their original victory in the Western Plaguelands.",al={"who rules andorhal","scourge in andorhal","who is in charge of andorhal"}},{q="Did Uther the Lightbringer die here?",a="Yes. Following the fall of Capital City, Uther and his paladins protected the urn containing King Terenas's ashes on the outskirts of Andorhal. Arthas assaulted the procession to seize the urn for Kel'Thuzad's remains, ultimately dueling and slaying his mentor on the road.",al={"where did uther die","uther death andorhal","did arthas kill uther here"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Andorhal is sacred ground consecrated by the blood of the Silver Hand; it was here that Uther the Lightbringer and his loyal knights made their tragic last stand against Arthas.",["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, the fields of Andorhal are a bitter reminder of their origin, marking the very place where the Cult of the Damned orchestrated the downfall of their mortal lives."},kw={"andorhal","grain","granary","kel'thuzad","plaguelands","scourge","uther"},rel={"zone:westernplaguelands","topic:scourge","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:arthas-menethil","subzone:stratholme","subzone:hearthglen","topic:lordaeron"},src={"Andorhal"}}
E["subzone:bulwark"] = {t="subzone",n="Bulwark",z="westernplaguelands",s="The Bulwark is a fortified outpost straddling the border between Tirisfal Glades and the Western Plaguelands. Maintained jointly by the Forsaken and the neutral Argent Dawn, it serves as the Horde's front line against the Scourge and the primary launching point for expeditions into the tainted east.",h="The Bulwark is a fortified outpost straddling the border between Tirisfal Glades and the Western Plaguelands.",sec={{t="A Shield Against the Scourge",b="Positioned squarely on the eastern border of Tirisfal Glades, the Bulwark is the final barrier protecting the Forsaken heartland from the blighted horrors of the Western Plaguelands. During the Scourging of Lordaeron, this ground served as an encampment led by Uther the Lightbringer to shelter survivors fleeing the ruins of Capital City. Today, rough wooden palisades and heavily armed guards defend the pass against roaming undead. It represents an uneasy but necessary collaboration, where Forsaken forces coordinate alongside the Argent Dawn to recruit seasoned champions of the Horde, turning back the minions of the Lich King before they spill into Tirisfal.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Bulwark?",a="The Bulwark serves as the Horde's first defensive garrison and staging ground against the Scourge. It protects Tirisfal Glades and the Undercity from incursions pouring out of the Western Plaguelands while housing Argent Dawn and Forsaken commanders who coordinate attacks into the blighted territory.",al={"why is bulwark here","purpose of bulwark","what is this place"},it="purpose"},{q="Can Alliance characters visit the Bulwark to speak with the Argent Dawn?",a="No. Although the Argent Dawn representatives stationed there are neutral, the encampment is heavily garrisoned by Forsaken guards. Any Alliance member who steps within the palisades will be attacked on sight.",al={"can alliance use bulwark","alliance at bulwark","is bulwark horde only"},gp=true},{q="What was here before the Forsaken built the fortification?",a="During the Third War and the fall of Capital City, Uther the Lightbringer established an emergency camp on this site to shelter and protect human survivors fleeing the Scourge's initial onslaught across Lordaeron.",al={"bulwark history","bulwark lore","uther camp"},it="history"},{q="Is the Bulwark safe for young adventurers leaving Tirisfal?",a="No. While the gate directly borders Tirisfal Glades, the Western Plaguelands beyond are filled with lethal, high-level undead and plague cauldrons. Novice travelers who wander past the palisade will face swift death.",al={"can low levels go past bulwark","entering plaguelands low level"},it="danger"},{q="What is the Bulwark?",a="The Bulwark is a fortified outpost standing on the border between Tirisfal Glades and the Western Plaguelands. It serves as the Forsaken's eastern defensive line, guarding the entrance to Tirisfal against the unrelenting horrors of the Scourge. Here, Forsaken sentries and emissaries of the Argent Dawn work side by side to coordinate defense, hold back undead incursions, and dispatch capable champions into the blighted lands beyond.",al={"what is bulwark","the bulwark overview","about the bulwark"},it="place",add=true},{q="Who occupies the Bulwark?",a="The outpost is manned by Forsaken soldiers pledged to Queen Sylvanas alongside members of the neutral Argent Dawn. While the Forsaken guard the border and secure their northern kingdom, the Argent Dawn maintains a presence here to recruit experienced adventurers from the Horde, supplying them with missions to strike against the Lich King's minions across the Plaguelands.",al={"who runs bulwark","who is at the bulwark","bulwark residents"},it="residents",add=true},{q="Why is it called the Bulwark?",a="The fortification takes its name from its role as a literal bulwark—a defensive rampart built to protect the Forsaken lands from invasion. Standing squarely at the threshold where Tirisfal meets the Plaguelands, its heavy barricades form the final line of defense holding back the monstrous legions of the Scourge.",al={"why called bulwark","bulwark name meaning","why the name bulwark"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, the Bulwark is the critical choke point that keeps the Lich King's armies from marching directly onto Brill and the Undercity.",["faction:Horde"]="The Bulwark acts as the Horde counterpart to the Alliance's Chillwind Camp, serving as the main entry gate and base of operations for operations across the Plaguelands."},kw={"argent dawn","bulwark","chillwind","forsaken","scourge","tirisfal","uther","western plaguelands"},rel={"zone:westernplaguelands","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:argent-dawn","topic:scourge","subzone:chillwind-camp","topic:horde","topic:lordaeron"},src={"Bulwark"}}
E["subzone:caer-darrow"] = {t="subzone",n="Caer Darrow",z="westernplaguelands",s="Caer Darrow is a fortified island situated in Darrowmere Lake in the Western Plaguelands. Formerly the aristocratic seat of the noble House Barov, the island's keep was secretly sold to Kel'Thuzad and transformed into the necromantic school of Scholomance, leaving the above-ground town plagued by wandering phantoms.",h="Caer Darrow is a fortified island situated in Darrowmere Lake in the Western Plaguelands.",sec={{t="A Legacy of War and Betrayal",b="Long before the Scourge, Caer Darrow was a strategic stronghold of Lordaeron that housed an ancient elven runestone. During the Second War, the Horde sacked the fortress and carved up the runestone to craft Gul'dan's Altars of Storms. When Lord Uther the Lightbringer sailed to offer relief to the survivors, his fleet was ambushed by Alterac privateers—an act of treachery that exposed Alterac's betrayal to the Alliance.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of House Barov",b="Rebuilt after the Second War, Caer Darrow served as the opulent estate of the Barov family, who held vast land deeds spanning Brill, Tarren Mill, and Southshore. Desperate to preserve their wealth and mortality as age encroached, the Barovs struck a bargain with Kel'Thuzad. The Cult of the Damned took root beneath the fortress, establishing Scholomance. Before the Third War even swept Lordaeron, a preliminary plague was loosed upon the island's servants and townsfolk. The populace perished, cursed to haunt the ruined keep as lingering echoes.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Caer Darrow?",a="Caer Darrow is an island fortress in Darrowmere Lake within the Western Plaguelands. Once the proud ancestral manor of the wealthy Barov family, it is now an eerie ruin whose catacombs house the necromantic academy known as Scholomance.",al={"caer darrow overview","what is this place","caer darrow lore"}},{q="Why is the town abandoned and ruined?",a="The Barov family bargained with Kel'Thuzad, trading their estate to the Cult of the Damned in exchange for immortality. The cult used the keep's servants for early necromantic experiments, eventually unleashing a fatal plague upon the entire settlement before the wider invasion of Lordaeron began.",al={"why is caer darrow destroyed","what happened to caer darrow","caer darrow history"}},{q="Why can't I see the ghosts in the town?",a="The murdered inhabitants of Caer Darrow linger as restless spirits caught in their final moments. They cannot be seen by normal eyes without special magical sight, such as using a Spectral Essence provided by the spirits of Eva and Lucien Sarkhoff.",al={"where are the ghosts","invisible people caer darrow","how to see ghosts"},sp=1},{q="What was the runestone that used to be here?",a="During the Second War, Caer Darrow housed an ancient high elven runestone. The Horde raided the island and chiseled the sacred stone into pieces, which Gul'dan used to construct the Altars of Storms to channel magic into ogre mages.",al={"runestone at caer darrow","elven runestone","altar of storms stone"}},{q="What is underneath the fortress?",a="Beneath the keep lies Scholomance, the headquarters and training academy of the Cult of the Damned. Led by Darkmaster Gandling, necromancers and acolytes inside study the dark arts and conduct experiments on the dead.",al={"what is under caer darrow","what is in the basement","dungeon under caer darrow"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="For the Alliance, Caer Darrow represents two profound betrayals: the ambush by Alterac privateers during the Second War that exposed King Perenolde's treason, and House Barov selling their ancestral home to Kel'Thuzad.",["race:Human"]="Human travelers will recognize the island as the former seat of House Barov, once one of Lordaeron's wealthiest noble families before their greed led them into pacts with the Cult of the Damned."},kw={"barov","caer darrow","cult of the damned","darrowmere lake","gandling","runestone","sarkhoff","scholomance","spectral essence"},rel={"subzone:scholomance","subzone:darrowmere-lake","zone:westernplaguelands","npc:eva-sarkhoff","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:alterac-kingdom","topic:second-war","npc:darkmaster-gandling"},src={"Caer Darrow"}}
E["subzone:chillwind-camp"] = {t="subzone",n="Chillwind Camp",z="westernplaguelands",s="Chillwind Camp sits at the southern pass leading into the Western Plaguelands from the Alterac Mountains. Manned by an Alliance contingent working alongside the Argent Dawn, it serves as the final safe staging ground before the blighted ruins of Lordaeron. From this precarious outpost, commanders direct strikes against the nearby Scourge strongholds and plague cauldrons.",h="Chillwind Camp sits at the southern pass leading into the Western Plaguelands from the Alterac Mountains.",sec={{t="The Edge of the Blight",b="Tucked into the rocky pass descending from Chillwind Point, Chillwind Camp is the first line of defense and primary base of operations for Alliance forces pushing into the Western Plaguelands. Though physically separated from the worst of the corruption by the high mountain ridges, the dead chill of the north hangs heavily over the tents and palisades. Under Commander Ashlam Valorfist, a sparse garrison of soldiers, scouts, and Argent Dawn specialists attempts to contain the horrors lingering along the King's Road and gather the intelligence necessary to reclaim Lordaeron's fallen fields.",sp=0},{t="A War of Attrition",b="Lacking the standing armies required for a full-scale assault on Andorhal or the necromantic fortress of Scholomance, Chillwind Camp relies heavily on independent champions and mercenaries. Operations here focus on surgical guerilla strikes: gathering field intelligence on the Cult of the Damned, neutralizing the alchemical plague cauldrons poisoning the soil, and keeping supply corridors open to eastern outposts like Light's Hope Chapel. With the Scourge occupying the ruins just to the north and the Forsaken fortifying the western border, the camp's defenders fight simply to keep the pass from being overwhelmed.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of Chillwind Camp?",a="Chillwind Camp serves as the primary southern foothold for the Alliance and the Argent Dawn at the border of the Western Plaguelands. It provides a secure base for staging guerrilla attacks against the Scourge in Andorhal and neutralizing the regional plague cauldrons.",al={"why is chillwind camp here","purpose of chillwind","what is chillwind camp"}},{q="How do you reach Chillwind Camp from Southshore?",a="From Southshore in the Hillsbrad Foothills, travel north along the road, head east at the crossroads, and follow the river northward into the Alterac mountain pass. Heading west through the high gap brings travelers out at Chillwind Point and down directly into the camp.",al={"how to get to chillwind camp","directions to chillwind","path to chillwind"}},{q="What is the Horde equivalent of this outpost?",a="The Horde's counterpart is the Bulwark, situated along the western frontier between Tirisfal Glades and the Western Plaguelands. Both outposts serve as fortified staging grounds for campaigns against the regional Scourge.",al={"horde chillwind camp","horde version of chillwind","is there a horde chillwind"}},{q="Who commands the forces at Chillwind Camp?",a="Commander Ashlam Valorfist leads the garrison at Chillwind Camp, coordinating Alliance volunteers, Knights of the Silver Hand, and Argent Dawn liaisons in operations across the surrounding ruins and tainted farms.",al={"chillwind camp leader","who is in charge of chillwind camp","commander of chillwind"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Chillwind Camp is the vanguard of the Alliance's effort to reclaim northern Lordaeron, offering safe refuge, griffon transit, and operational orders against the Scourge.",["class:Paladin"]="Promising paladins are directed here by Lord Grayson Shadowbreaker in Stormwind to assist Commander Valorfist and bring the holy wrath of the Silver Hand to the undead ranks."},kw={"alterac mountains","andorhal","argent dawn","ashlam valorfist","cauldrons","chillwind camp","chillwind point","western plaguelands"},rel={"zone:westernplaguelands","subzone:chillwind-point","subzone:andorhal","subzone:bulwark","subzone:scholomance","subzone:light-s-hope-chapel","topic:argent-dawn","npc:lord-grayson-shadowbreaker"},src={"Chillwind Camp"}}
E["subzone:dalson-s-tears"] = {t="subzone",n="Dalson's Tears",z="westernplaguelands",s="Dalson's Tears is a blighted farmstead located in the heart of the Western Plaguelands. Once known as Dalson's Orchard, the land was overrun during the Third War and converted into a staging ground for one of the Scourge's noxious plague cauldrons. The undead remains of the former owners still haunt the fields and outbuildings.",h="Dalson's Tears is a blighted farmstead located in the heart of the Western Plaguelands.",sec={{t="A Poisoned Harvest",b="Before the Third War, this estate flourished as Dalson's Orchard, a productive farm in the breadbasket of Lordaeron. Today, the fields are choked with decay and rechristened Dalson's Tears. At the center of the ruined property stands a massive iron cauldron, constantly billowing virulent green plague vapors across the landscape. The device is overseen by Cauldron Lord Malvinious and defended by a pack of blighted zombies, rotting cadavers, and skeletal horrors serving the Lich King.",sp=0},{t="The Fate of the Dalsons",b="The farm's original occupants met a tragic end when the plague swept through the region, yet death brought no release. Mrs. Dalson wanders the poisoned soil as a mindless skeleton, clutching a key to the farm's outhouse. Inside that small shack lurks the horrifying ghoul that was once Farmer Dalson himself, locked away by his desperate wife before both succumbed completely to the Scourge's curse.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What was Dalson's Tears called before the plague?",a="Before the Third War devastated Lordaeron, this property was a prosperous agricultural plot known as Dalson's Orchard.",al={"dalson's orchard","original name","what was this farm called"}},{q="Why is there a giant cauldron in the middle of the farm?",a="The Scourge erected massive plague cauldrons across Lordaeron's farmland to continuously boil virulent toxins. The vapors drift across the region, blighting the earth, poisoning water supplies, and corrupting any living creature exposed to them.",al={"dalson cauldron","green smoke kettle","why is there a cauldron here"}},{q="Who guards the cauldron at Dalson's Tears?",a="The plague cauldron is protected by Cauldron Lord Malvinious, an undead commander serving the Scourge, flanked by hordes of blighted zombies, skeletal terrors, and rotting cadavers.",al={"cauldron lord","who is the boss here","cauldron lord malvinious"}},{q="Who is locked inside the outhouse?",a="Farmer Dalson is trapped inside the outhouse. As the plague took hold of him, his wife locked him inside, where he ultimately transformed into a ravenous ghoul.",al={"outhouse monster","who is in the outhouse","farmer dalson"},sp=1}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The ruined homestead is a stark reminder of the fate shared by all people of Lordaeron before Sylvanas Windrunner broke the Lich King's control over the Forsaken.",["class:Paladin"]="The desecrated fields and suffering souls of the Dalson family demonstrate the lingering evil that holy warriors of the Light are sworn to cleanse from Lordaeron."},kw={"cauldron lord malvinious","dalson's orchard","dalson's tears","farmer dalson","mrs dalson","plague cauldron","western plaguelands"},rel={"zone:westernplaguelands","subzone:cauldron","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","topic:light"},src={"Dalson's Tears"}}
E["subzone:darrowmere-lake"] = {t="subzone",n="Darrowmere Lake",z="westernplaguelands",s="Darrowmere Lake is an expansive freshwater lake situated in the southeastern reaches of the Western Plaguelands. At its heart lies the fortress island of Caer Darrow, whose ruined halls conceal the Cult of the Damned's dread necromantic academy, Scholomance.",h="Darrowmere Lake is an expansive freshwater lake situated in the southeastern reaches of the Western...",sec={{t="A Storied Inland Sea",b="Darrowmere Lake is among the largest inland bodies of water in the Eastern Kingdoms, bordered by the blight-stricken forests of Lordaeron and fed by the Thondroril and Darrowmere rivers. During the Second War, these deep waters saw intense naval skirmishes between the Horde and Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore's fleet. Proudmoore's transports ferried Alliance leaders Turalyon and Alleria Windrunner through the lake to relieve Stratholme, and it was along these shores that the forest troll warlord Zul'jin was captured. Once a thriving waterway and trade artery for the House of Barov, the lake is now a grim, mist-veiled basin in the heart of the Plaguelands.",sp=0},{t="The Shadow on the Waters",b="Dominating the center of the lake is Caer Darrow, once the proud ancestral seat of the Barov family. Beneath its broken foundations lies Scholomance, the Scourge's primary academy for necromancy and dark arts. The waters surrounding the island are stagnant and fraught with danger, reflecting the dark legacy of the Cult of the Damned. Adventurers venturing across its surface must brave not only the perils of the blighted countryside but the pervasive dread cast by the fortress at its core.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is Darrowmere Lake located?",a="Darrowmere Lake is located in the southeastern corner of the Western Plaguelands, connecting the Thondroril River to the north and the Darrowmere River flowing to the south.",al={"darrowmere location","where is darrowmere lake","how to get to darrowmere"}},{q="What is on the island in the center of the lake?",a="The island holds Caer Darrow, an ancient settlement once ruled by the noble House of Barov. Beneath the ruined town lies Scholomance, a sprawling school of dark magic operated by the Cult of the Damned.",al={"island in darrowmere","what is on the island","caer darrow location"}},{q="What role did Darrowmere Lake play in the Second War?",a="The lake was a contested waterway where the Proudmoore fleet fought naval battles against the Horde. Transports used the lake to ferry Turalyon and Alleria's forces toward Stratholme, and the troll warlord Zul'jin was captured near its shores.",al={"second war darrowmere","naval battles darrowmere","darrowmere history"}},{q="Can ships still sail into Darrowmere Lake?",a="While massive naval fleets navigated the lake during the Second War, the connecting rivers now feature steep waterfalls and shallow passages, preventing large vessels from sailing in from the sea.",al={"darrowmere boat access","can ships reach darrowmere"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="For Alliance veterans, Darrowmere Lake is remembered as a key naval front where Proudmoore's fleet fought to halt the Old Horde and relieve Stratholme during the Second War.",["race:Undead"]="The lake marks the threshold of Caer Darrow, where the living Barovs bargained away their mortal home to the Lich King to create the dread academy of Scholomance."},kw={"barov","caer darrow","darrowmere","darrowmere lake","proudmoore fleet","scholomance","thondroril river","western plaguelands"},rel={"zone:westernplaguelands","subzone:caer-darrow","subzone:scholomance","subzone:thondroril-river","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:lordaeron","topic:second-war","subzone:stratholme"},src={"Darrowmere Lake"}}
E["subzone:felstone-field"] = {t="subzone",n="Felstone Field",z="westernplaguelands",s="Felstone Field is a blighted farmstead located in the northwestern Western Plaguelands. Once a thriving piece of Lordaeron agricultural land, it has been claimed by the Scourge and anchored by a plague cauldron spewing toxic fumes across the ruined soil.",h="Felstone Field is a blighted farmstead located in the northwestern Western Plaguelands.",sec={{t="A Blighted Homestead",b="Before the Third War, Felstone Field was a peaceful, productive family farm feeding the citizens of Lordaeron. When the plague swept through the north, the homestead fell swiftly to the Cult of the Damned and the Scourge. Today, the fields sprout dead weeds and creeping rot instead of grain. At the heart of the farmland sits an enormous plague cauldron, continuously churning foul vapors into the sky under the watch of Cauldron Lord Bilemaw and legions of skeletal flayers, sorcerers, and ravenous ghouls.",sp=0},{t="Lingering Ghosts and Zealots",b="Despite the undead occupation, traces of the living world remain trapped in the ruins. Inside the northeastern farmhouse lingers the ghost of Janice Felstone, wandering the shell of her former home in sorrow. Meanwhile, patrols from the nearby Scarlet Crusade prowl the borders of the property, violently purging any undead they cross while striking down any outsiders who venture too close to their crusade.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the cauldron in the center of the field doing?",a="The cauldron is a Scourge plague engine designed to saturate the surrounding land and atmosphere with pestilence, corrupting the soil and raising the dead. It is guarded by a powerful Scourge overseer, Cauldron Lord Bilemaw, along with skeletal minions and ghouls.",al={"felstone cauldron","why is there a cauldron","plague cauldron felstone"}},{q="Who is the ghost inside the farmhouse?",a="The ghost is Janice Felstone, the former mistress of the farmstead who died when the Scourge overran the territory. Unwilling or unable to rest, her spirit remains tied to the ruins of her family's house.",al={"janice felstone","ghost in felstone house","who is in the house"}},{q="Why are there Scarlet Crusaders patrolling the farm?",a="The Scarlet Crusade frequently dispatches aggressive strike teams into the Western Plaguelands to destroy the Scourge. They treat any trespassing travelers, Horde or Alliance, as potential plague-bearers or enemies, attacking everything on sight.",al={"scarlet crusade felstone","crusaders at felstone field"}},{q="Who actually owns the deed to Felstone Field?",a="Both Jeremiah Payson in the Undercity and Ol' Emma in Stormwind claim to have received the deed from their sister, Janice Felstone. It remains a mystery whether two deeds were drafted or if the chaos of the plague left inheritance hopelessly tangled between living and undead family members.",al={"felstone field deed","who owns felstone","better late than never deed"},sp=1}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The ruined farm stands as a stark reminder of the lives Forsaken once led in Lordaeron, brought home by relatives like Jeremiah Payson who survived only through undeath.",["faction:Alliance"]="For Alliance survivors in Stormwind, sites like Felstone Field represent lost ancestral lands and displaced kin such as Ol' Emma, who clings to claims of a ruined northern inheritance."},kw={"cauldron lord bilemaw","felstone field","janice felstone","jeremiah payson","lordaeron","ol' emma","plague cauldron","scarlet crusade","western plaguelands"},rel={"zone:westernplaguelands","subzone:cauldron","topic:scourge","topic:scarlet-crusade","topic:alliance","topic:horde"},src={"Felstone Field"}}
E["subzone:gahrron-s-withering"] = {t="subzone",n="Gahrron's Withering",z="westernplaguelands",s="Gahrron's Withering is a corrupted farmstead nestled along the eastern border of the Western Plaguelands near the path to the Eastern Plaguelands. Once a thriving piece of Lordaeron farmland called Gahrron's Quickening, it is now occupied by the Scourge and anchored by a massive cauldron pumping virulent plague vapors into the air.",h="Gahrron's Withering is a corrupted farmstead nestled along the eastern border of the Western Plaguelands near...",sec={{t="From Quickening to Decay",b="Before the Third War ruined the kingdom of Lordaeron, this estate was known as Gahrron's Quickening, named after its master, Del Gahrron. Its fertile fields yielded grain for the realm until the Cult of the Damned and the Scourge swept across the land. Renamed Gahrron's Withering in bitter mockery of its past vitality, the homestead now stands broken and derelict. Haunting visions, wailing wraiths, and restless spirits wander the ruined outbuildings, overseen by Scourge overseers sworn to the Lich King.",sp=0},{t="The Cauldron and the Fall of Redpath",b="In the center of the blighted fields sits one of the Scourge's massive plague cauldrons, tended by Cauldron Lord Soulwrath. This cauldron constantly spews noxious miasma into the surrounding atmosphere, poisoning the earth and driving off natural life. Beyond its role in spreading pestilence, the farm marks the tragic climax of the nearby tragedy of Darrowshire: it was here that Alliance defenders finally confronted and struck down Captain Joseph Redpath after he succumbed to dark corruption. The warrior's shattered shield was left lying amidst the cursed soil.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What was Gahrron's Withering called before the plague?",a="Before the fall of Lordaeron, the farm was known as Gahrron's Quickening. It was an active homestead owned by Del Gahrron before the Scourge devastated the countryside.",al={"original name","old name","gahrrons quickening"}},{q="Why is there a giant cauldron here?",a="The cauldron is an engine of pestilence deployed by the Scourge to spread plague vapors across the region. Cauldron Lord Soulwrath protects the apparatus to ensure it continuously blights the soil and air.",al={"plague cauldron","what does the cauldron do","pot in field"}},{q="Who was Joseph Redpath and why does he matter here?",a="Captain Joseph Redpath was the defender of nearby Darrowshire who was corrupted during the Scourge's slaughter of the town. Alliance forces finally cornered and slew his corrupted form at this farmstead, leaving his shield behind in the dirt.",al={"redpath shield","captain redpath","darrowshire connection"},sp=1},{q="Who controls Gahrron's Withering right now?",a="The farm is firmly held by the Scourge. It is haunted by spectral wraiths, wailing deaths, and haunting visions, all commanded by the powerful necromantic officer Cauldron Lord Soulwrath.",al={"who is here","mobs at gahrron","scourge in charge"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="For Alliance veterans, the farm holds grim significance as the place where the fallen hero of Darrowshire, Joseph Redpath, had to be put down by his own countrymen.",["class:Paladin"]="The vile fumes pouring from Cauldron Lord Soulwrath's cauldron and the restless dead lingering on the ruined fields make this farm a priority target for holy cleansing."},kw={"cauldron","darrowshire","del gahrron","gahrron","gahrron's withering","joseph redpath","quickening","soulwrath"},rel={"subzone:cauldron","subzone:darrowshire","subzone:chillwind-camp","zone:westernplaguelands","topic:scourge","topic:argent-dawn","topic:alliance","topic:lich-king"},src={"Gahrron's Withering"}}
E["subzone:hearthglen"] = {t="subzone",n="Hearthglen",z="westernplaguelands",s="Tucked away in the northern hills of the Western Plaguelands, Hearthglen is a fortified settlement that remarkably escaped complete physical destruction during the fall of Lordaeron. Today, its sturdy stone walls, towers, and surrounding farmlands serve as the primary northern stronghold and training ground for the fanatical Scarlet Crusade.",h="Tucked away in the northern hills of the Western Plaguelands, Hearthglen is a fortified settlement that...",sec={{t="A Bastion in the Blight",b="While neighboring Andorhal lies shattered in skeletal ruin, Hearthglen still resembles a living town of Lordaeron, complete with a lumber mill, scout towers, stables, and a functioning mine. Historically governed from Mardenholde Keep by Lord Tirion Fordring, Hearthglen was renowned for its prosperity and staunch defense during the Second War. When the Plague of Undeath swept Lordaeron during the Third War, Prince Arthas Menethil intercepted tainted grain here, purging the infected townsfolk and repelling a massive Scourge siege alongside Uther the Lightbringer. Through that desperate intervention, Hearthglen's formidable walls survived intact.",sp=0},{t="Under the Scarlet Banner",b="After the Silver Hand fell into disarray following the kingdom's collapse, Tirion's son, Taelan Fordring, opened Hearthglen as a sanctuary for surviving paladins. However, the grief and zealotry that birthed the Scarlet Crusade soon consumed the fortress. Now, Hearthglen is a fortress of religious extremism. Scarlet crusaders patrol its approaches and man its ramparts, violently executing any outsider, living or undead, who trespasses on their land. Highlord Taelan Fordring rules from Mardenholde Keep, a devout servant to the Crusade's uncompromising war against the Plaguelands.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Hearthglen look so clean compared to the rest of the Plaguelands?",a="Hearthglen was spared utter ruin during the Third War thanks to Prince Arthas and Uther the Lightbringer, who arrived just in time to slay early plague victims and break a massive Scourge siege. Afterward, the Scarlet Crusade heavily fortified the pass and valley, continually purging any encroaching Scourge before the rot could take over the town.",al={"why is hearthglen intact","hearthglen not destroyed","hearthglen alive"}},{q="Who controls Hearthglen right now?",a="The Scarlet Crusade controls Hearthglen. It is commanded from Mardenholde Keep by Highlord Taelan Fordring, son of the former lord and famed paladin Tirion Fordring.",al={"who rules hearthglen","hearthglen leader","who is in charge of hearthglen"}},{q="Is Hearthglen friendly to Alliance or Horde visitors?",a="No. The Scarlet Crusade views all outsiders with extreme hostility and paranoia. Horde members are treated as monstrous abominations, and unaligned Alliance travelers are condemned as plague-carriers or heretics, marked for immediate execution on sight.",al={"can i go to hearthglen","is hearthglen hostile","hearthglen safe"}},{q="What happened to Tirion Fordring?",a="Years before the Third War, Tirion was stripped of his title and excommunicated by the Alliance for defending an honorable orc named Eitrigg. Exiled to the wilds, Tirion was forced to leave Hearthglen behind, and command of Mardenholde eventually passed to his son, Taelan.",al={"where is tirion","tirion hearthglen history","why isn't tirion here"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Hearthglen was once home to Tirion Fordring and served as a rallying haven for the remnants of the Silver Hand after Lordaeron fell. Walking its approaches reveals how tragic the order's splintering into the fanatical Scarlet Crusade truly became.",["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, Hearthglen is a lethal thorn in the north. The crusaders stationed here harbor absolute, burning hatred for the undead and actively patrol to eradicate any scouts venturing north from Andorhal."},kw={"andorhal","hearthglen","mardenholde","scarlet crusade","taelan fordring","tirion fordring","western plaguelands"},rel={"subzone:mardenholde-keep","zone:westernplaguelands","npc:tirion-fordring","topic:scarlet-crusade","subzone:andorhal","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:plague-of-undeath","npc:eitrigg"},src={"Hearthglen"}}
E["subzone:mardenholde-keep"] = {t="subzone",n="Mardenholde Keep",z="westernplaguelands",s="Mardenholde Keep is the imposing stone citadel overlooking Hearthglen in the northern reaches of the Western Plaguelands. Once the ancestral seat of Highlord Tirion Fordring, it now serves as the regional command center for the fanatical Scarlet Crusade under the rule of his son, Highlord Taelan Fordring.",h="Mardenholde Keep is the imposing stone citadel overlooking Hearthglen in the northern reaches of the Western...",sec={{t="Seat of the Fordrings",b="Built as the administrative heart of the Hearthglen principality, Mardenholde Keep was long celebrated by the people of Lordaeron for having never breached during the Second War. From its council chambers, Highlord Tirion Fordring governed the surrounding vales alongside his trusted advisors, maintaining peace until his controversial trial and exile for defending the orc Eitrigg. Following Tirion's fall, governance fell to his ambitious apprentice Barthilas and eventually passed to Tirion's son, Taelan, who inherited his father's domain on the eve of Lordaeron's destruction.",sp=0},{t="Cradle of the Crusade",b="When the Scourge swept through Lordaeron, Taelan opened Mardenholde to surviving commanders of the Knights of the Silver Hand, including Alexandros Mograine and Saidan Dathrohan. Within these halls, the remnants of the order plotted their resistance against the undead. However, internal paranoia and dark subversion soon fractured their fellowship. When accusations of treachery surfaced following Mograine's demise, Maxwell Tyrosus broke away to found the Argent Dawn, while Dathrohan and the remaining zealots reorganized Hearthglen into a fortified bastion of the newly minted Scarlet Crusade.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who controls Mardenholde Keep right now?",a="The keep is held by the Scarlet Crusade and serves as their primary military command in the Western Plaguelands. It is governed directly by Highlord Taelan Fordring, who resides in the main hall surrounded by elite Scarlet protectors.",al={"who leads mardenholde","who is in charge of hearthglen keep","mardenholde boss"}},{q="Why is this fortress so important to Tirion Fordring?",a="Mardenholde was Tirion Fordring's ancestral home and the seat from which he ruled the region as an Alliance paladin. He raised his family within its walls until he was stripped of his titles and exiled for defending an honorable enemy.",al={"tirion connection to mardenholde","did tirion live here"}},{q="How did the Scarlet Crusade take over Mardenholde?",a="After Tirion's exile and the fall of Lordaeron during the Third War, Tirion's son Taelan offered the keep as a refuge for surviving Silver Hand paladins. Influenced by commanders like Saidan Dathrohan, Taelan pledged Hearthglen's garrison to their radical new order.",al={"how did scarlet crusade get hearthglen","why is scarlet crusade here"}},{q="Can players enter Mardenholde Keep safely?",a="No. The Scarlet Crusade considers all outsiders to be potential carriers of the plague or enemies of their cause. Any adventurer approaching the keep will be attacked on sight unless disguised through powerful illusion magic.",al={"is mardenholde friendly","can alliance enter mardenholde","can horde enter mardenholde"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Mardenholde represents a tragic chapter in paladin history: once a noble sanctuary of the Silver Hand, its halls were where the order splintered between the fanatical Scarlet Crusade and the vigilant Argent Dawn.",["race:Human"]="For humans of Lordaeron, Mardenholde stands as a bitter reminder of how their homeland's proudest strongholds survived the orcish wars only to succumb to madness and zealotry during the Scourge."},kw={"dathrohan","hearthglen","mardenholde","scarlet crusade","silver hand","taelan fordring","tirion fordring"},rel={"subzone:hearthglen","npc:tirion-fordring","topic:scarlet-crusade","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:argent-dawn","zone:westernplaguelands","topic:lordaeron","npc:eitrigg"},src={"Mardenholde Keep"}}
E["subzone:northridge-lumber-camp"] = {t="subzone",n="Northridge Lumber Camp",z="westernplaguelands",s="Northridge Lumber Camp is an active lumber mill nestled in the northern hills of the Western Plaguelands near Hearthglen. Currently held and fortified by the Scarlet Crusade, it provides timber vital to sustaining their nearby strongholds. Both the Alliance and the Horde view the facility as a prime target to cripple the zealots' regional infrastructure.",h="Northridge Lumber Camp is an active lumber mill nestled in the northern hills of the Western Plaguelands near...",sec={{t="Fueling the Crusade",b="Situated just down the road from Hearthglen, the Northridge Lumber Camp serves as a vital logistics node for the Scarlet Crusade. Despite the lingering plague choking the surrounding forests, the camp fells and processes regional timber to construct fortifications, repair watchtowers, and furnish the crusade's war effort in Lordaeron. Heavily garrisoned by fanatical laborers and guards, the mill is fiercely protected against both the Scourge and outside trespassers.",sp=0},{t="Target for Sabotage",b="Because Hearthglen depends heavily on this lumber to maintain its defenses, outside forces actively conspire to disrupt it. Both Alliance commanders and Horde scouts recognize that an overt assault on fortified crusader positions carries high costs. Instead, agents look to unconventional sabotage—such as unleashing voracious wood-boring termites into the mill's lumber stores—to quietly rot the Crusade's supplies from the inside out.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who controls Northridge Lumber Camp?",a="The Scarlet Crusade currently controls and operates the lumber camp, using its guards and laborers to harvest timber for nearby Hearthglen.",al={"who runs northridge","who controls the lumber mill","northridge faction"}},{q="Why is this lumber mill important?",a="It supplies essential timber and building materials to maintain the Scarlet Crusade's fortifications and outposts throughout the northern Western Plaguelands.",al={"why does northridge matter","significance of northridge","what is this camp for"}},{q="Why do the Alliance and Horde want to sabotage it?",a="Both factions view the aggressive and fanatical Scarlet Crusade as a dangerous threat in the Plaguelands. Depriving them of lumber weakens their ability to fortify Hearthglen without requiring a costly direct invasion.",al={"why attack northridge","termites quest","why ruin the lumber"}},{q="Who ran the mill before the Third War?",a="Before the Kingdom of Lordaeron fell to the Scourge, local civilian families such as the Dumahs and Levines lived and worked the mill as a quiet regional enterprise.",al={"who owned northridge lumber camp","history of northridge mill"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Though the Scarlet Crusade claims to fight the undead, their violent xenophobia makes them a menace to Alliance reconnaissance and regional operations in northern Lordaeron.",["faction:Horde"]="For the Forsaken and their Horde allies, disrupting the crusade's lumber operations weakens Hearthglen and pushes zealots further back toward their strongholds."},kw={"dumah","hearthglen","levine","lumber","mill","northridge","plaguelands","scarlet crusade","termites"},rel={"subzone:hearthglen","topic:scarlet-crusade","zone:westernplaguelands","topic:alliance","topic:horde","topic:third-war"},src={"Northridge Lumber Camp"}}
E["subzone:plaguemist-ravine"] = {t="subzone",n="Plaguemist Ravine",z="westernplaguelands",s="Plaguemist Ravine is a narrow mountain passage connecting the verdant wilderness of the Hinterlands to the haunted shores of Darrowmere Lake in the Western Plaguelands. Named for the sickly fog that rolls down from the north, it provides a quiet and desolate route across the mountain range.",h="Plaguemist Ravine is a narrow mountain passage connecting the verdant wilderness of the Hinterlands to the...",sec={{t="A Blighted Threshold",b="Tucked away in the mountains northwest of Quel'Danil Lodge, Plaguemist Ravine acts as a geographic threshold between two radically different realms. To the south lies the lush, untamed highland forest of the Hinterlands, while to the north the pass opens directly upon Caer Darrow and the blighted expanse of the Western Plaguelands. The ravine earned its name from the foul, unnatural vapors that creep southward out of the Plaguelands, creating a jarring contrast with the crisp mountain air of the neighboring wilderness.",sp=0},{t="A Quiet Passage",b="Despite cutting directly into the edge of Scourge-ravaged territory, the ravine itself remains remarkably deserted. Travelers find neither wild beasts nor wandering undead lingering within the gorge itself. Because of this eerie stillness, the winding path serves as a convenient, albeit ominous, back road for daring couriers and adventurers wishing to bypass the heavily fortified highways and border checkpoints leading into Lordaeron.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where does Plaguemist Ravine lead?",a="Plaguemist Ravine links the Hinterlands to the Western Plaguelands. Heading north brings you out to the shores of Darrowmere Lake near the island fortress of Caer Darrow, while heading south deposits you into the forested hills northwest of Quel'Danil Lodge.",al={"where does plaguemist ravine go","plaguemist ravine path","plaguemist connection"}},{q="Why is it called Plaguemist Ravine?",a="The pass was named recently due to the pervasive, blighted fog that drifts south out of the Western Plaguelands. The sickly air stands in sharp contrast to the fresh, uncorrupted atmosphere typical of the Hinterlands.",al={"why is it called plaguemist","plaguemist name origin"}},{q="Are there any enemies or NPCs in the ravine?",a="No. The ravine is entirely empty of hostile creatures, wildlife, and friendly non-player characters, making it an unusually quiet passage between two active zones.",al={"plaguemist ravine mobs","is plaguemist ravine safe"}},{q="How do you find the entrance in the Hinterlands?",a="In the Hinterlands, the entrance lies tucked in the northern mountain wall west of Quel'Danil Lodge and north of Rhapsody Shindigger's campsite.",al={"how to find plaguemist ravine","plaguemist ravine entrance"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="The ravine serves as a peaceful transition point, though its lack of game or woodland beasts makes it an eerie border for trackers moving north into the Plaguelands.",["faction:Alliance"]="The pass provides Alliance scouts stationed at Quel'Danil Lodge a discreet route to monitor the blighted shores around Caer Darrow without facing open road patrols."},kw={"caer darrow","darrowmere","hinterlands","mountain pass","plaguemist","plaguemist ravine","quel'danil","western plaguelands"},rel={"zone:westernplaguelands","zone:hinterlands","subzone:caer-darrow","quest:938"},src={"Plaguemist Ravine"}}
E["subzone:ruins-of-andorhal"] = {t="subzone",n="Ruins of Andorhal",z="westernplaguelands",s="Once the agricultural hub and grain distribution center of northern Lordaeron, the Ruins of Andorhal now lie at the heart of the Western Plaguelands as a devastated Necropolis stronghold. Under the dominion of the lich Araj the Summoner, the shattered township crawls with legions of undead abominations, ghouls, and wraiths.",h="Once the agricultural hub and grain distribution center of northern Lordaeron, the Ruins of Andorhal now lie...",sec={{t="Heart of the Plague",b="Andorhal was the initial staging ground for the plague of undeath in Lordaeron, where grain tainted by the Cult of the Damned was packed and distributed across the human kingdom. Today, the ruined city serves as a sprawling bastion of the Scourge in the Western Plaguelands. Araj the Summoner presides over the center of the ruins, flanked by ghouls, skeletal horrors, and towering flesh golems. Scouting parties of the Scarlet Crusade periodically probe the city's outskirts, but the Scourge maintains iron control over the shattered streets.",sp=0},{t="Unraveling Time",b="Beyond the physical presence of the undead, Andorhal suffers from peculiar temporal anomalies. The town hall clock and discarded pocket watches scattered through the streets remain perpetually stopped at three o'clock. The bronze dragon Chromie, sheltering in the inn on the edge of the ruins, investigates temporal parasites nesting within the grain silos. Scourge forces appear to be meddling with the flow of time itself, attempting to alter the timeline of the city's tragic downfall.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who rules the Ruins of Andorhal?",a="The ruins are governed by Araj the Summoner, a powerful lich who coordinates Scourge activities in the area. He resides in the city center, surrounded by an elite vanguard of skeletal guards and flesh constructs.",al={"who leads andorhal","andorhal boss","araj the summoner"}},{q="Why is Chromie inside the ruins?",a="Chromie, taking the guise of a female gnome, observes and seeks to repair unnatural disturbances in the timeline. The Scourge forces here are actively breeding temporal parasites inside the city silos, seeking to disrupt historical events.",al={"what is chromie doing in andorhal","why is chromie here","chromie andorhal"}},{q="Why are all the clocks stopped at three o'clock?",a="The town hall clock and the personal watches found among the debris are locked at precisely three o'clock. This temporal freezing is tied to the unnatural time manipulation and temporal parasites warping reality throughout the district.",al={"three oclock watch","andorhal clock frozen","why stopped at 3"}},{q="Why does the Scarlet Crusade attack Andorhal?",a="Small strike teams consisting of Scarlet Medics, Hunters, and Invokers attempt to infiltrate the city. The fanatical crusade seeks to reclaim Lordaeron and extinguish the Scourge, though their incursions into the fortified center rarely succeed.",al={"scarlet crusade in andorhal","scarlet patrols andorhal"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="For the Knights of the Silver Hand and paladins across the Alliance, Andorhal is consecrated ground turned nightmare—the very site where the plague poisoned Lordaeron's grain stores and doomed the northern kingdom.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken travelers view the ruined city with grim remembrance: many who walk the Undercity today were citizens of Lordaeron who originally ingested the poisoned grain harvested from these surrounding fields."},kw={"andorhal","araj the summoner","chromie","grain silos","scourge","temporal parasites","western plaguelands"},rel={"subzone:andorhal","zone:westernplaguelands","topic:scourge"},src={"Ruins of Andorhal"}}
E["subzone:scholomance"] = {t="subzone",n="Scholomance",z="westernplaguelands",s="Hidden deep within the crypts beneath Caer Darrow, Scholomance is the Scourge's premier academy of dark arts and necromancy. Here, mortals and undead alike study the Plague of Undeath and fleshcrafting under the sadistic guidance of Darkmaster Gandling and the watchful gaze of the lich Ras Frostwhisper.",h="Hidden deep within the crypts beneath Caer Darrow, Scholomance is the Scourge's premier academy of dark arts...",sec={{t="A Pact of Ruin",b="Before the Third War, Caer Darrow was the proud seat of House Barov, a wealthy aristocratic dynasty holding vast lands across Lordaeron, including Brill, Tarren Mill, and Southshore. Fearing the loss of their wealth and mortality, the Barovs succumbed to Kel'Thuzad's seductive promises of eternal life. They deeded their fortress and ancestral catacombs to the Cult of the Damned, who promptly repaid the nobles by butchering them and turning the sprawling crypts into a hidden necromantic training ground.",sp=0},{t="The School of Necromancy",b="Beneath the haunted surface ruins of Caer Darrow lies a thriving center of Scourge industry. Within these damp halls, early plague strains were tested on living servants, and the first hideous abominations were stitched together. Today, mortal acolytes flock here to hone their mastery over dark magic, supervised by Headmaster Darkmaster Gandling and the icy sentinel Ras Frostwhisper, while the restless undead remains of the Barov family linger in eternal torment.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Scholomance?",a="Scholomance is a secret academy of necromancy run by the Cult of the Damned and the Scourge. Located beneath the island fortress of Caer Darrow, it serves as a training facility for mortal necromancers, plague researchers, and fleshcrafters.",al={"what is this place","scholomance lore","explain scholomance"}},{q="Who owns Caer Darrow and the crypts?",a="The keep originally belonged to House Barov, one of the wealthiest noble families in Lordaeron. Seeking immortality, they surrendered their ancestral home to Kel'Thuzad, only to be murdered and raised as undead thralls within their own halls.",al={"who owned this","house barov","barov family"}},{q="Who runs the academy now?",a="Darkmaster Gandling serves as the school's insidious mortal headmaster, overseeing instruction and torment. The site is also heavily guarded on behalf of the Scourge by the lich Ras Frostwhisper.",al={"who is the boss","who leads scholomance","headmaster"}},{q="What horrors were developed here?",a="Scholomance was an early crucible for the Cult of the Damned. The cultists tested prototype variants of the Plague of Undeath on the Barov household servants and stitched together the Scourge's first flesh abominations within its laboratories.",al={"what was made here","experiments in scholomance","abominations"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="The twisted lessons taught inside Scholomance represent the absolute perversion of arcane scholarship, where inquiry into magical theory has been replaced with rot, shadow, and unholy desecration.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken spellcasters view Scholomance with cold familiarity and disgust; many of the Scourge's foulest methods of enslavement and biological warfare were perfected in these very classrooms."},kw={"barov","caer darrow","cult of the damned","darkmaster gandling","kel'thuzad","necromancy","ras frostwhisper","scholomance"},rel={"subzone:caer-darrow","npc:darkmaster-gandling","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:scourge","topic:plague-of-undeath","zone:westernplaguelands","npc:eva-sarkhoff","topic:lordaeron"},src={"Scholomance"}}
E["subzone:scholomance-classic"] = {t="subzone",n="Scholomance (Classic)",z="westernplaguelands",s="Scholomance is a subterranean academy of necromancy hidden in the crypts beneath Caer Darrow in the Western Plaguelands. Founded by Kel'Thuzad and the Cult of the Damned, it serves as the Scourge's premier training ground for mortal spellcasters seeking to master death.",h="Scholomance is a subterranean academy of necromancy hidden in the crypts beneath Caer Darrow in the Western...",sec={{t="A School in the Tombs",b="Before the Third War devastated Lordaeron, the rogue archmage Kel'Thuzad sought recruits for his Cult of the Damned. He found willing benefactors in the wealthy Barov family, lords of Caer Darrow, who traded their lands and crypts in exchange for promised immortality. Instead, the cult slaughtered them and raised them as monstrous undead. Beneath the ruined fortress, the Cult renovated the ancient Arathorian catacombs into an academy for the dark arts. Here, mortal acolytes study plaguecraft, soul binding, and flesh shaping away from watchful eyes, honing the foul magics that shattered Lordaeron.",sp=0},{t="Lich and Headmaster",b="Though Kel'Thuzad has moved on to direct Scourge operations from above, the school remains fully operational. The eternal lich Ras Frostwhisper holds vigil over the complex in the Scourge's name, while the mortal necromancer Darkmaster Gandling serves as its headmaster, tutoring ambitious sorcerers in horrific experiments. Those students who display weakness are discarded into the catacombs and fed to the Ravenian, a voracious wight lurking in the lower vaults.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Scholomance?",a="Scholomance is a secret academy of necromancy run by the Cult of the Damned. Situated in the deep crypts beneath the island fortress of Caer Darrow, it trains mortal spellcasters in the dark arts of the Scourge.",al={"what is this place","scholomance lore","explain scholomance"}},{q="Who rules Scholomance now?",a="The lich Ras Frostwhisper oversees the fortress on behalf of the Scourge, but mortal affairs and instruction are directed by Darkmaster Gandling, the school's ruthless headmaster.",al={"who leads scholomance","who is in charge","scholomance boss"}},{q="How did the Scourge acquire Caer Darrow?",a="The noble Barov family held the keep following the Second War. Swayed by Kel'Thuzad's promises of eternal life, they deeded their ancestral home and crypts to the Cult of the Damned. The cult fulfilled the bargain by slaughtering the Barovs and raising them into undeath.",al={"barov backstory","who owned caer darrow","how did cult get scholomance"}},{q="Why is the school locked?",a="The massive iron gate was sealed to protect the academy's research and summoners from intruders. Opening the door requires the Skeleton Key, forged through an involved ritual process spanning the Plaguelands.",al={"scholomance door locked","how to get into scholomance","skeleton key scholomance"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Scholomance is a grotesque perversion of Dalaran's scholastic traditions, demonstrating what happens when Kirin Tor expertise is turned wholly to necromancy and decay.",["class:Paladin"]="For knights of the Silver Hand, cleansing the tainted halls beneath Caer Darrow is an urgent duty, striking directly at the source of the Scourge's mortal leadership."},kw={"barov","caer darrow","cult of the damned","gandling","necromancy","ras frostwhisper","scholomance"},rel={"subzone:caer-darrow","npc:darkmaster-gandling","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:scourge","zone:westernplaguelands","subzone:chillwind-camp","subzone:bulwark"},src={"Scholomance (Classic)"}}
E["subzone:sorrow-hill"] = {t="subzone",n="Sorrow Hill",z="westernplaguelands",s="Sorrow Hill is an expansive graveyard resting south of Andorhal in the Western Plaguelands. Once the final resting place for commoners, soldiers, and nobility alike, it has been corrupted by nearby plague cauldrons into a sprawling haven of restless undead.",h="Sorrow Hill is an expansive graveyard resting south of Andorhal in the Western Plaguelands.",sec={{t="A Burden of Graves",b="Before the Third War, Sorrow Hill served as the primary burial ground for the city of Andorhal and its surrounding townships. When the Scourge unleashed the Plague of Undeath, the gravediggers could not keep pace with the dead. Neatly kept crypts were soon surrounded by shallow, poorly marked earthen pits. Eventually, terrified survivors ceased traditional burials entirely, instead resorting to burning the corpses of their loved ones in a mass pyre to the south out of fear they would rise. Before the town fell completely, the remnants of the populace entombed Lord Uther the Lightbringer atop the cemetery's high ground, bestowing upon the hill its tragic name.",sp=0},{t="The Resurrected Dead",b="The Scourge's foul plague cauldrons across the region have long since corrupted Sorrow Hill. The interred soldiers and citizens now wander the mist-shrouded headstones as skeletal flayers and ravenous ghouls, while skeletal mages and a local Scourge commander nest in the southeastern crypts. Because the cemetery sits directly along the road between Chillwind Camp and Andorhal, both the Alliance and the Argent Dawn frequently order patrols to culled the ranks of undead here to maintain a vital foothold in the region.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why was this graveyard named Sorrow Hill?",a="It earned the name during the fall of Lordaeron, when the surviving people of Andorhal suffered catastrophic losses to the Scourge. Their final collective act before fleeing was laying their fallen champion, Lord Uther the Lightbringer, to rest in a grand tomb atop the hill.",al={"sorrow hill name meaning","why is it called sorrow hill"}},{q="Why are there so many shallow graves here?",a="When the Plague of Undeath struck Andorhal, deaths mounted so quickly that citizens had no time to construct proper tombs. They dug hasty, poorly marked plots until the onslaught became so overwhelming that they abandoned burial altogether in favor of mass burning.",al={"shallow graves sorrow hill","why are graves poorly marked"}},{q="Who is buried in Sorrow Hill?",a="Beyond thousands of fallen soldiers and citizens of Andorhal, the cemetery houses the great tomb of Uther the Lightbringer. It also features the resting place of the cannoneer Dargal and a monument honoring Joseph Redpath, whose body was destroyed.",al={"famous graves sorrow hill","notable people buried here"}},{q="What threat does Sorrow Hill pose today?",a="Corrupted by the Lich King's plague cauldrons, the cemetery's dead have risen as skeletal flayers, slavering ghouls, and skeletal mages. Led by a Scourge commander residing within the southeastern crypt, these undead threaten the southern approach to Andorhal.",al={"scourge at sorrow hill","who controls sorrow hill"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Sorrow Hill is a place of profound mourning and reverence for paladins, who come to pay homage at Uther's Tomb while driving back the undead desecrating the grounds around their mentor.",["faction:Alliance"]="Positioned just down the road from Chillwind Camp, the cemetery represents both a strategic checkpoint toward Andorhal and the sacred resting place of Lordaeron's fallen defenders."},kw={"andorhal cemetery","dargal","joseph redpath","skeletal flayers","sorrow hill","uther's tomb","western plaguelands"},rel={"subzone:uther-s-tomb","subzone:andorhal","subzone:chillwind-camp","zone:westernplaguelands","topic:plague-of-undeath","subzone:cauldron","topic:argent-dawn","topic:scourge"},src={"Sorrow Hill"}}
E["subzone:uther-s-tomb"] = {t="subzone",n="Uther's Tomb",z="westernplaguelands",s="Uther's Tomb is the sacred resting place of Uther the Lightbringer, situated atop Sorrow Hill in the Western Plaguelands. Constructed by his surviving followers after his defeat at nearby Andorhal, this monument stands as one of the few remaining bastions of the Holy Light amidst a blighted and haunted region.",h="Uther's Tomb is the sacred resting place of Uther the Lightbringer, situated atop Sorrow Hill in the Western...",sec={{t="A Bastion in the Blight",b="Perched south of the ruins of Andorhal at Sorrow Hill, Uther's Tomb marks the final resting place of the legendary paladin who founded the Knights of the Silver Hand. Following the collapse of Lordaeron, Uther made his last stand in Andorhal, defending the royal urn holding the ashes of King Terenas Menethil II against Prince Arthas Menethil and the Scourge. Arthas struck down his former mentor and desecrated the urn to recover Kel'Thuzad's remains. Surviving soldiers salvaged Uther's body, laying him to rest within a stone sanctuary. Despite the relentless corruption engulfing the surrounding lands, the tomb remains miraculously untouched by decay, held under the steadfast vigil of High Priest Thel'danis.",sp=0},{t="The Lightbringer's Legacy",b="Approached via the Path of Uther, the monument centers on a grand stone effigy depicting the paladin with a tome of the Holy Light in one hand and his warhammer in the other. Pilgrims who travel these treacherous roads frequently seek out the tomb to pay homage, pray, or reflect upon the devotion that defined the first paladins. Even amid the horrors of the Plaguelands, faithful visitors who show reverence before Uther's likeness often find themselves touched by a lingering aura of grace and resolve.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is buried in this tomb?",a="Uther the Lightbringer is entombed here. He was the supreme commander and founder of the Knights of the Silver Hand, the first paladin order in human history, and served as Prince Arthas Menethil's mentor before Lordaeron fell to the Scourge.",al={"who is uther","who is buried here","uther the lightbringer"}},{q="How did Uther die?",a="Uther was slain in Andorhal by his former student, Arthas Menethil, who had returned from Northrend as a death knight. Uther fell while fiercely defending the urn containing the ashes of Arthas's father, King Terenas Menethil II.",al={"who killed uther","how did uther die","arthas kills uther"}},{q="Who is High Priest Thel'danis?",a="High Priest Thel'danis is the devoted caretaker of the monument. He maintains a quiet, sacred vigil over Uther's resting place, braving the surrounding undead to ensure the shrine remains a sanctuary of the Light.",al={"theldanis","priest at uther's tomb","who protects the tomb"}},{q="Why isn't the tomb corrupted by the plague?",a="The persistent holiness of Uther's memory and the enduring power of the Holy Light consecrate the grounds. While the surrounding forests of Sorrow Hill have withered and fallen to rot, the shrine itself remains an unbroken sanctuary against the blight.",al={"why is uther's tomb clean","why no plague at tomb"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Standing before Uther's statue is a profound pilgrimage for any paladin, honoring the father of their order whose sacrifice embodies the virtues of the Silver Hand.",["faction:Alliance"]="The tomb serves as an enduring testament to Lordaeron's fallen legacy and a place of quiet reflection for Alliance travelers braving the horrors of the Plaguelands."},kw={"andorhal","arthas","lightbringer","silver hand","sorrow hill","theldanis","uther"},rel={"subzone:sorrow-hill","subzone:ruins-of-andorhal","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:terenas-menethil-ii","topic:light","zone:westernplaguelands"},src={"Uther's Tomb"}}
E["subzone:weeping-cave"] = {t="subzone",n="Weeping Cave",z="westernplaguelands",s="The Weeping Cave is a foul, subterranean cavern tucked into the hills of the northeastern Western Plaguelands, just west of the Thondroril River. Once a natural cave system, it now seeps with toxic blight and festers with grotesque fungal beasts and oozes.",h="The Weeping Cave is a foul, subterranean cavern tucked into the hills of the northeastern Western...",sec={{t="A Den of Rot",b="Carved into the rugged bluffs above the Thondroril River, the Weeping Cave earned its name from the continual trickling of moisture through its rocky ceiling—water that has long since turned rank and corrupt. The Scourge's plague has thoroughly permeated the soil above, draining virulent filth down into the dark. Within, rotting horrors, decaying behemoths, and caustic oozes wander the damp tunnels, accompanied by an enigmatic corrupted elemental entity known simply as The Husk.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Weeping Cave located?",a="The Weeping Cave is situated in the northeastern corner of the Western Plaguelands, nestled in the rocky ridges just west of the Thondroril River near the border to the Eastern Plaguelands.",al={"weeping cave location","where is weeping cave","find weeping cave"}},{q="What creatures dwell inside the cave?",a="The cavern is infested with shambling plant-like abominations—such as rotting behemoths and decaying horrors—as well as toxic creeping slimes and the rare, tormented elemental known as The Husk.",al={"weeping cave monsters","what is inside weeping cave","weeping cave mobs"}},{q="Why is it called the Weeping Cave?",a="The cavern's ceiling continually drips with moisture, giving the appearance of stone that weeps. In the aftermath of the plague, however, the runoff has turned into a rancid, putrid seepage that fosters virulent fungal growth.",al={"why is it called weeping cave","weeping cave name meaning"}},{q="Who is The Husk?",a="The Husk is a rare corrupted elemental creature found deep within the Weeping Cave, warped and twisted by the lingering alchemical blight that has infected the surrounding earth and water.",al={"the husk","the husk weeping cave","husk rare"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The Weeping Cave represents a grotesque perversion of the natural world, where subterranean flora and elemental energies have been thoroughly corrupted by the plague of undeath into violent monstrosities."},kw={"decaying horror","ooze","rotting behemoth","the husk","thondroril river","weeping cave","western plaguelands"},rel={"zone:westernplaguelands","subzone:thondroril-river"},src={"Weeping Cave"}}
E["subzone:writhing-haunt"] = {t="subzone",n="Writhing Haunt",z="westernplaguelands",s="The Writhing Haunt is a blighted farmstead situated east of the Ruins of Andorhal in the Western Plaguelands. Once fertile land that fed Lordaeron, it has been seized by the Scourge and transformed into a toxic spreading ground for plague vapors.",h="The Writhing Haunt is a blighted farmstead situated east of the Ruins of Andorhal in the Western Plaguelands.",sec={{t="A Field of Disease",b="Like many of the old human homesteads surrounding Andorhal, the farmstead now called the Writhing Haunt fell swiftly during the Third War. Today, the fields do not grow crops but instead harvest rot. The Scourge planted an enormous plague cauldron squarely in the center of the tilled earth, continuously venting noxious, diseased vapors into the air. Rotting ghouls, freezing ghouls, and fetid zombies wander the overgrown furrows, guarding the machinery under the command of Cauldron Lord Razarch.",sp=0},{t="A Watcher in the Ruins",b="Despite the omnipresent danger of the Scourge and the choking miasma from the cauldron, the farm is not entirely abandoned by the living. Tucked away inside one of the dilapidated farmhouses hides Mulgris Deepriver. Seeking a way to neutralize the spreading contagion and strike back against the plague apparatus, he observes the cauldron's operation and awaits capable travelers willing to confront Razarch and disrupt the Scourge's foul enterprise.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What was the Writhing Haunt before the plague?",a="Before the Third War, the Writhing Haunt was a productive, life-sustaining human farm that helped supply grain and livestock to nearby Andorhal and the greater Kingdom of Lordaeron.",al={"writhing haunt history","what was this farm","old farm andorhal"}},{q="Why is there a giant cauldron in the middle of the field?",a="The cauldron was placed by the Scourge to actively brew and disperse plague vapors across the surrounding countryside, poisoning the soil, the water, and any living creatures nearby.",al={"plague cauldron writhing haunt","why cauldron here","green smoke farm"}},{q="Who controls the Writhing Haunt?",a="The site is commanded by Cauldron Lord Razarch, an undead servant of the Scourge who guards the plague cauldron alongside a host of ghouls and zombies.",al={"who is in charge writhing haunt","writhing haunt boss","razarch"}},{q="Who is hiding in the farmhouse?",a="Mulgris Deepriver is sheltered inside the abandoned farmhouse, monitoring the cauldron and coordinating efforts to dismantle the Scourge's operations in the area.",al={"npc in house","mulgris deepriver","who is mulgris"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The desecration of rich farming soil by plague vapors makes the Writhing Haunt an active offense against the natural order, demanding that the cauldron be silenced.",["faction:Alliance"]="Seeing the farmland outside Andorhal reduced to a diseased wasteland under Scourge rule is a bitter reminder of the fall of Lordaeron."},kw={"andorhal","cauldron lord razarch","mulgris deepriver","plague cauldron","western plaguelands","writhing haunt"},rel={"zone:westernplaguelands","subzone:ruins-of-andorhal","topic:scourge","subzone:cauldron","subzone:andorhal"},src={"Writhing Haunt"}}
E["zone:westernplaguelands"] = {t="zone",n="Western Plaguelands",z="westernplaguelands",s="Once the fertile agricultural breadbasket of northern Lordaeron, the Western Plaguelands now choke beneath a toxic mist and legions of undead. While Kel'Thuzad's cauldrons ruined the region's farms and towns, the soil still clings tenaciously to life. The Argent Dawn, the Alliance, the Forsaken, and the Scarlet Crusade all wage desperate campaigns across its blighted fields.",h="Once the fertile agricultural breadbasket of northern Lordaeron, the Western Plaguelands now choke beneath a...",sec={{t="The Breadbasket of Lordaeron",b="Before the Third War, this land was a verdant corridor of farms stretching east from Tirisfal Glades toward Darrowmere Lake. Hearthglen governed the northern administration from Mardenholde Keep, while the sprawling town of Andorhal orchestrated the storage and export of grain across the kingdom. That crucial agricultural role made the region Kel'Thuzad's primary target. By introducing the Plague of Undeath directly into the grain shipments at Andorhal, the Cult of the Damned turned Lordaeron's sustenance into its doom.",sp=0},{t="A Land Under Siege",b="To permanently poison the soil, the Scourge raised four massive plague cauldrons across the largest agricultural estates: Felstone Field, Dalson's Tears, the Writhing Haunt, and Gahrron's Withering. Today, monstrous Cauldron Lords maintain these noxious vats, pumping venomous fog into the atmosphere. The surrounding trees twist with swollen, tumorous growths, and blighted wildlife rampages through the brush. Beneath Caer Darrow on Darrowmere Lake, Darkmaster Gandling trains necromancers within Scholomance, compounding the undead threat.",sp=0},{t="Divided Factions",b="Despite the omnipresence of the Scourge, several factions maintain embattled footholds. The Scarlet Crusade controls the fortress of Hearthglen in the north, purging anything they deem tainted with rabid zeal. To the south, Alliance forces stage from Chillwind Camp, while the Forsaken defend their borders at the Bulwark. Mediating and directing the fight against the undead cauldrons is the neutral Argent Dawn, which aids champions of both factions and guards Uther's Tomb—the last truly sanctified ground in the region.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why did the Plague hit this area so hard?",a="Andorhal was the agricultural shipping hub of northern Lordaeron. Kel'Thuzad intentionally targeted the regional supply, infecting grain silos and installing massive plague cauldrons across four major farmsteads to rapidly distribute undeath through food shipments across the kingdom.",al={"why is western plaguelands plagued","what happened to andorhal","why cauldrons here"}},{q="Who controls Hearthglen in the north?",a="Hearthglen and its fortress, Mardenholde Keep, are ruled by the Scarlet Crusade. Though originally a regional administrative hub for Lordaeron, the zealots now execute any outsiders who approach their borders, viewing all non-crusaders with suspicion.",al={"hearthglen faction","who lives in mardenholde keep","is hearthglen friendly"}},{q="Where can Alliance and Horde players find safe outposts?",a="The Alliance maintains Chillwind Camp near the southern border with the Alterac Mountains, providing a flight master and Argent Dawn allies. The Horde operates from the Bulwark on the western border entering from Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where are the flight paths","horde camp wpl","alliance camp wpl"}},{q="What is the purpose of the plague cauldrons in the fields?",a="Erected by the Cult of the Damned at Felstone Field, Dalson's Tears, the Writhing Haunt, and Gahrron's Withering, these cauldrons pump constant plague vapors into the air and soil. Argent Dawn agents recruit adventurers to slay the Cauldron Lords and counter-engineer the toxic brews.",al={"what are the cauldrons","cauldron purpose","felstone cauldron"}},{q="What is Scholomance and where is it located?",a="Scholomance is a subterranean academy of necromancy run by the Scourge and the Cult of the Damned. It is hidden beneath the ruins of Caer Darrow, an island fortress situated in the southeastern waters of Darrowmere Lake.",al={"scholomance location","where is scholomance","caer darrow dungeon"}},{q="Is any part of the Western Plaguelands uncorrupted?",a="Uther's Tomb, located on Sorrow Hill near the southern edge of the zone, remains entirely holy and untouched by blight. It stands as a consecrated memorial to the fallen High General Uther the Lightbringer and the lost Order of the Silver Hand.",al={"holy ground in wpl","is uther tomb clean","uther's tomb safe"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Chillwind Camp serves as the forward Alliance garrison, where Commander Ashlam Valorfist directs campaigns to disrupt the plague cauldrons and avenge the fallen cities of Lordaeron.",["faction:Horde"]="The Bulwark marks the frontier of Forsaken sovereignty; from here, the Horde pushes eastward to neutralize Scourge commanders and contest Scarlet zealots along their vulnerable flank.",["class:Paladin"]="The desecrated fields of Lordaeron are a somber trial for holy warriors, offering pilgrimage to Uther's Tomb as well as righteous war against the horrors of Scholomance and Hearthglen's corrupted zealots.",["profession:Herbalism"]="The lingering taint makes the region one of the few places where rare, dangerous flora like Plaguebloom and Arthas' Tears can take root among blighted pine groves."},kw={"andorhal","bulwark","caer darrow","cauldrons","chillwind camp","hearthglen","mardenholde keep","scholomance","uther's tomb","western plaguelands"},rel={"zone:easternplaguelands","subzone:ruins-of-andorhal","subzone:scholomance","subzone:hearthglen","subzone:uther-s-tomb","subzone:bulwark","subzone:chillwind-camp","topic:scourge"},src={"Western Plaguelands (Classic)","Western Plaguelands"}}
